# Trie and state roots

**`mpt` is a node tree, and it memoizes.** `foundation/trie/types.lisp` retains a
`root` node beside the entry table, and every node caches its RLP object, its
encoding, its reference and its hash. `mpt-put` and `mpt-delete` rewrite only the
nodes on one root-to-leaf path (`trie-put-node`, `trie-delete-node`) and retain
the untouched subtrees. Nodes are immutable once built, so there is no
invalidation protocol to get wrong: a node that was not replaced still has a
valid cache.

A root therefore costs the touched paths, not the whole state. Measured in the
warm image: changing one leaf re-encodes 5 nodes at 512 entries and 5 nodes at
16384, while cold builds of those same tries cost 548 and 17477.

**Do not turn this back into a rebuild.** Two tests exist to stop that, and they
cover different layers. `trie-rehash-cost-is-independent-of-trie-size` guards the
trie module. `account-trie-flush-cost-is-independent-of-account-count` guards
`flush-account-trie`, which is where the per-block cost actually lives — a flush
that stopped applying only the dirty accounts leaves the trie-level test green.
Both compare two sizes rather than bounding one size, because a rebuild of a
small enough tree satisfies any fixed bound.

**The root oracle is opt-in, not ambient.** `*verify-incremental-root*` compares
every flush against a full rebuild, and it defaults to nil. Only
`tests/state-account-trie-cache-tests.lisp` binds it true, through
`with-verified-account-root`; the suite as a whole does not, despite older notes
saying otherwise. Binding it true across the unit and integration layers passes
and costs nothing measurable, but that has not been made the default and the e2e
layer is unmeasured. Anything that counts node encodings must bind it nil
explicitly, since the oracle rebuilds the world on purpose.

**Memoization did not remove materialization.** A root over a lazily-backed
`state-db` still loads every account, because `state-db-state-trie` calls
`state-db-materialize`. Reading one account loads one account; taking a root
loads all of them. Cheap re-rooting is not a bounded-memory import.

**Settled — do not re-litigate without new measurements.** `state-db-copy` is not
a bottleneck, at 0.05–0.75ms, despite deep-cloning the world on every CALL/CREATE
through snapshots. It has already been suspected once and refuted by measurement.
